-- An upsert on slug, so re-running it refreshes the text and keeps every id.
insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('speaking-at-a-funeral', 'Speaking at a Funeral', 'You have been asked to say something, you want to do it, and you are terrified of breaking down in front of everyone. How to write it, how to get through it, and what to do if you cry.', 'Somebody asked you, or you offered, and now there is a page to write and a room to stand up in. You want to do it properly. You are also lying awake imagining your voice going, or forgetting how to read, or standing there unable to speak while a church full of people waits.

Two difficult things are happening at once: public speaking, which frightens most people, and grief, which has taken your concentration and your composure. It is reasonable to find this hard.

Write it for one person

A eulogy that tries to summarize a whole life gets long and strangely flat. The ones people remember are specific.

So pick three or four things: one story that shows who they were, one thing they said that everyone in the room will recognize, one small ordinary detail, and one sentence about what they meant to you.

Write it the way you talk. Read it out loud as you go, which is the only way to find the sentences you cannot say.

Keep it short. Three to five minutes is plenty, and in a hard room short is kinder to everyone, including you.

Make the page easy to read

This part matters more than people expect, because grief and adrenaline make reading harder than usual.

Large print, double spaced, one side of several pages rather than tiny text. Do not staple them. Number the pages in case they go out of order. Mark where to pause with a slash.

Print two copies, give one to somebody else, and have it on paper rather than on a phone that can lock or run out of battery.

Practice until it is boring

Read it out loud, standing up, at least five or six times, including once to a person. The first two readings will probably undo you, and that is the point. The crying tends to move to the rehearsals if you let it, which leaves you steadier on the day.

Mark the two or three lines that break you every time and consider rewriting them slightly shorter or plainer. The feeling stays and the sentence becomes sayable.

Plan for crying, because it is likely

The fear is almost always about losing control in front of people. So decide in advance what you will do, and then it is not a catastrophe but a plan.

Stop. Put a hand on the lectern. Look down at the page. Breathe out slowly. Count three seconds. Drink the water you put there beforehand. Then carry on.

Say the line if you need to: give me a moment. Nobody in that room will think anything other than that you loved them, and most of them are crying as well. Crying at a funeral is not a failure of composure, it is the correct response to the situation.

Have a hand-off ready

Ask one person in advance to be your backup, with a copy in their pocket, and agree the signal: if I nod at you, come and finish it.

Knowing there is an exit is usually what makes the exit unnecessary. People who have a backup almost always get through it themselves.

Practical things that help on the day

Water on the lectern. Something to hold. Tissues in your pocket, not your bag. Flat shoes. Being near the front so the walk is short. Someone sitting where you can see them.

Pick one friendly face in the middle distance and talk to them rather than scanning the room, which is what tips people over. Looking at a grieving relative''s face mid-sentence is the most common cause of losing it.

Slow down more than feels natural. Nerves make everyone speed up, and the slowing is what keeps your voice steady.

Be careful with alcohol and medication. A drink to steady yourself tends to loosen the tears rather than hold them, and anything new on the day is a gamble. If you take something prescribed, a conversation with your doctor beforehand is better than borrowing from a friend.

Afterward, expect a crash

People describe feeling oddly flat or exhausted once they sit down, and sometimes tearful hours later when it is over. You have been holding a great deal together to do a job.

Let somebody look after you for the rest of the day. The performance part is done, and the grief is still there underneath it, which is usually what arrives next.

If you cannot do it, say so

Being asked is an honor and it is not an obligation. If you know it will be beyond you, tell the family early so they can ask someone else, and offer something you can manage instead: writing it for another person to read, choosing the music, reading one short poem rather than speaking about them.

Declining is not letting anyone down. Agreeing and then being unable to is harder for everybody, including you.

When to get help

Please talk to your doctor or a therapist if the dread of speaking in public is affecting much more of your life than this one occasion, since social anxiety responds well to treatment. And if the grief itself is not easing after months, if you are not sleeping, or if you are drinking more to get through the evenings, that is worth taking to a professional rather than waiting it out. If you have had thoughts of not wanting to be here, please tell someone today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Social Anxiety', 'Grief']::text[], 0, '2026-10-28T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('moving-a-body-that-sits-all-day', 'Moving a Body That Sits All Day', 'Nine hours in a chair leaves you stiff, achy and oddly tired. What sitting actually does, and the small movements through the day that undo most of it.', 'You get up from the desk at six and your hips do not want to straighten. Your neck aches on one side. Your lower back has that dull tightness that was not there at nine in the morning, and despite having done nothing physical you feel wrung out.

Then the advice arrives: you should exercise more. Which may be true and is not the main thing going on here.

What sitting does

Long stretches of sitting are not harmful because sitting is bad. They are a problem because nothing changes. Muscles held in one position get stiff and shortened, the ones that should be working switch off, circulation slows, and your spine stays in one shape for hours.

The result is predictable: tight hips and hamstrings, a rounded upper back, a neck doing the work of holding your head forward, and a lower back that aches without ever being injured.

And the tiredness is real. Being still for nine hours leaves most people less alert than moving does, which is why the afternoon slump is partly a movement problem rather than only a sleep one.

Frequency beats intensity here

This is the key point. An hour at the gym after work is good for you, and it does not undo nine hours of stillness. What undoes stillness is interrupting it.

Standing up and moving for one or two minutes every half hour or so does more for stiffness, circulation and alertness than one long session. Any excuse will do: fill the water glass, take the call standing, walk to the printer, go to a different floor for the bathroom, stand up whenever the phone rings.

Set something to prompt you, because nobody remembers. A repeating alarm, a smartwatch nudge, or a glass of water small enough that refilling it is the reminder.

The four movements worth knowing

If you only do a handful of things, these address what sitting actually does.

Open the front of the hips. Stand up, step one foot back, keep yourself upright and press the back hip forward gently. Twenty or thirty seconds each side. This is the single most useful one for a stiff lower back.

Open the chest and shoulders. Stand in a doorway, forearms on the frame, step through until you feel the stretch across the chest. Or clasp your hands behind your back and lift them slightly.

Move your spine. Sitting or standing, round the lower back and then arch it slowly, eight or ten times. Then turn gently to each side.

Chin and neck. Instead of stretching the neck, slide your chin straight back as though making a double chin, hold a few seconds, repeat ten times. That counters the forward head position.

None of this requires a mat, a change of clothes, or anyone watching.

Set the chair up once, properly

A ten-minute setup beats years of adjusting your body to bad furniture.

Feet flat on the floor or a box, knees roughly level with hips. Screen at eye height so you are not looking down, which is where neck pain comes from. Keyboard close enough that your elbows stay by your sides. The back of the chair actually supporting your lower back, with a rolled towel behind it if it does not.

If you use a laptop all day, raise the screen and add a separate keyboard. It is the cheapest fix available for neck and shoulder pain.

Standing is not the answer by itself

Standing desks help some people and standing still for six hours brings its own aches, in the feet, knees and lower back. The useful version is alternating: a while sitting, a while standing, moving between the two.

If you have one, change position every half hour rather than committing to a whole morning upright.

Walk at the edges of the day

A walk before work and a walk after it do more than people expect, partly for the stiffness and partly because they bracket the day. Ten minutes each is enough to matter.

Stairs are the most efficient thing available to most desk workers: one flight, twice a day, gets you breathing harder than a corridor walk ever will.

Build the strength that sitting takes away

Over years, sitting leaves the hips, back and shoulders weak as well as stiff, and weakness is what turns into pain. Two short sessions a week of simple strength work changes that: sitting down and standing up from a chair slowly, pushing away from a wall, going up onto your toes, holding a plank position.

Ten minutes, twice a week, with no equipment. The point is not fitness, it is being able to sit all day without hurting.

Treat pain as information rather than something to push through

Aches that come and go with position are usually about stillness and setup. Pain that is worsening, that wakes you at night, that travels down an arm or a leg, or that comes with numbness, weakness or pins and needles is different and should be looked at rather than stretched.

When to get help

Please see your doctor or a physical therapist if back, neck or shoulder pain has lasted more than a few weeks, is getting worse, wakes you at night, or comes with numbness, tingling or weakness anywhere. A physical therapist can look at how you sit and give you the three movements that fit your particular body, which beats any general list. If your employer has a workplace assessment available, ask for one, since getting the desk right is usually cheaper and more effective than treating what it does to you.', array['Movement', 'Body']::text[], 0, '2026-10-28T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-worth-rides-on-your-output', 'When Your Worth Rides on Your Output', 'A good day makes you an acceptable person and a slow one makes you worthless. Why tying your worth to what you produce never pays off, and what to build underneath it instead.', 'On a productive day you feel briefly fine. Not happy exactly, but permitted. On a day where you got little done, something worse happens: not disappointment about the work, but a drop in how you regard yourself as a person.

That is the giveaway. Most people are annoyed by an unproductive day. If an unproductive day makes you feel worthless, your self-worth is attached to your output, and that attachment is doing you harm whatever it is producing.

Why it never settles

The arrangement has a built-in flaw: nothing you achieve can be banked.

The relief after finishing something lasts a day or two at most. Then the standard moves, the achievement becomes the new baseline, and anything less than it feels like decline. People describe it as a treadmill, and that is accurate, because the ground keeps moving and standing still counts as falling behind.

It also means you cannot enjoy what you have done. The evening you finish the thing, you are already thinking about the next one, and the people around you are confused about why you are not pleased.

Where it usually comes from

Almost nobody chooses this. It is learned, usually early.

Houses where attention and approval arrived with achievement and not much otherwise. A parent who was proud of results. School where you were the clever one, and that became the whole of your identity. Work that rewarded output and said nothing about anything else. Or a long stretch where producing was genuinely how you kept yourself safe.

Seen that way it is not a flaw in you, it is a rule you absorbed when it was useful, and which has outlived its usefulness.

Notice the tax it is charging

It helps to look squarely at what it costs, because the arrangement is usually defended on the grounds that it makes you effective.

Rest becomes impossible to enjoy, because resting feels like losing ground. Illness becomes frightening rather than inconvenient. Holidays are uncomfortable. You cannot accept help, because needing it is evidence. You judge other people by their usefulness, which makes you harder to be around than you think. And eventually it produces burnout, because there is no amount of output that lets you stop.

It is also, quietly, not very productive. People running on this tend to work longer and worse, avoid difficult tasks where they might fail visibly, and lose whole days to the dread of starting.

Separate the two judgments on purpose

The work is not to stop caring about doing things well. It is to stop one judgment from carrying the other.

So practice saying both parts separately. Today was a slow day and I am the same person I was on Tuesday. That piece of work was not good enough and nothing about my worth was decided by it.

This feels like a trick at first. Said often enough, it starts to be how you actually think, because what you are doing is unpicking a habit of inference rather than talking yourself into something.

Build something underneath that is not output

If achievement is the only pillar, every bad week threatens the whole structure. The fix is more pillars, deliberately.

The way you treat people. Someone you are reliably there for. Something you do badly and enjoy anyway. A place you belong where nobody knows what you do for a living. A kindness nobody saw.

These feel insubstantial compared to accomplishments, and they are what holds people steady through illness, redundancy and the years when nothing much is achieved.

Test the belief rather than arguing with it

The belief predicts something: that if you produce less, people will value you less.

So check it. Do a smaller amount for a week. Say no to something. Leave on time. Tell a colleague you had an unproductive day. Then notice what actually happens, which in almost every case is nothing.

The deeper version is worth checking too: ask one person who loves you what they would say they value about you. Almost nobody answers with your output, and hearing that from a real person does more than any amount of private reasoning.

Practice resting badly

Rest, for someone in this pattern, is the hardest skill. Start small and expect discomfort: an evening with nothing planned, a Sunday with no list, an hour doing something with no outcome.

The discomfort is not a sign it is wrong. It is withdrawal from a rule, and it passes as rest stops needing to be earned.

Watch for the collapse point

The most dangerous moment is failure, since a person whose worth is the work has nowhere to stand when the work goes badly. A lost job, a project that fails, a bad review, or retirement can produce a drop out of proportion to the event.

If you are near that, this is urgent rather than interesting: the pillars are worth building before you need them.

When to get help

Please talk to a therapist if a bad day at work reliably becomes a verdict on you as a person, if you cannot rest without guilt, or if you are heading toward burnout on this. It is one of the most common things people bring, and it responds well, particularly where it goes back to how you were raised. Please tell your doctor too if you are exhausted, not sleeping, or low for weeks, and if a failure or an ending has left you feeling worthless in a way that frightens you, treat that as a reason to get help now rather than later.', array['Perfectionism', 'Self-Esteem']::text[], 0, '2026-10-28T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-someone-tells-you-something-heavy', 'When Someone Tells You Something Heavy', 'A friend tells you what happened to them, and you freeze. What actually helps in that conversation, what not to say, and how to carry it afterward without it flattening you.', 'It comes out suddenly, usually sideways, usually at the end of an ordinary evening. Something that happened to them years ago, or last month. Abuse, an assault, a death they have never described, a thing they have told nobody.

And you sit there with your heart going, aware that whatever you say next matters, and completely unsure what it should be.

You do not have to fix it

The commonest mistake is reaching for a solution, because the discomfort of just listening is considerable. But nobody tells you something like this because they want it solved in the next ten minutes.

What they want is almost always simpler: to be believed, and to find out that telling you did not cost them the relationship.

So the most useful thing you can do is stay in the room, stay steady, and let them talk at the speed they are talking.

The sentences that help

Short and plain beats eloquent. These do most of the work.

Thank you for telling me.

I believe you.

That should not have happened to you.

I am glad you are still here.

What would help right now, me listening or me saying something.

Then be quiet and let the silence sit. People filling silences is why a lot of these conversations go wrong.

The ones that land badly

Not because the speaker is unkind, but because of what they do to the person telling you.

Why did you not tell anyone, or why did you stay, which both sound like questions about their judgment.

At least it was not worse. Everything happens for a reason. You need to forgive them to move on. Time heals.

My cousin went through the same thing, and then twenty minutes about your cousin.

I always knew there was something wrong with that man, which turns it into your insight.

And the one that does the most damage: pushing them to report it, confront someone, or tell a family member, right now, when they have just told you.

Ask before you act

This is the piece people get wrong with the best intentions. Having heard something awful, you want to do something, and you start suggesting police, lawyers, phone calls, telling a parent.

Control is often exactly what was taken from them. So ask rather than organize. Would you like me to help you look into what the options are. Would you like me to come with you if you ever want to talk to someone. And then respect a no, including a no that lasts years.

The exception is a child or someone who cannot protect themselves being at risk now, or someone in immediate danger. There, you do have to act, and it is better to say so plainly: I have to tell someone about this because a child is involved, and I will tell you exactly what I am doing.

Do not make them manage your reaction

Crying, going pale, getting furious, or saying I cannot bear to think about it puts the person who was harmed in the position of looking after you. Many people decide never to tell anyone again after that experience.

Feel all of it, and feel most of it later. In the moment, your job is to be the steady one for half an hour.

Ask what they need afterward, in specifics

Do you want me to check in about this, or would you rather I never mention it. Is it all right to ask how you are. Is there anything you want me to not do.

That conversation is worth more than any promise of support, because it tells you what being helpful looks like for this particular person.

Keep it

Unless someone is in danger, what they told you is theirs. Not for your partner, not for the family group chat, not for the friend who knows them.

If you need to talk it through, and you may well, do it with a therapist or a helpline rather than someone who knows them.

Then look after yourself, because this does land on you

Hearing something terrible about someone you love has a weight. People commonly feel shaken for days, sleep badly, find the images intrusive, or feel guilty for not having known.

That is a normal response to secondhand horror rather than weakness. Take it seriously: get sleep, move, talk to somebody outside the situation, and give yourself a few days of lower demands.

If it stirred up something of your own, which is common, that is worth attention in its own right rather than being pushed down because this is not about you.

When to get help

Please encourage them toward a therapist who works with trauma when they are ready, and offer practical help with finding one rather than a push to go. Say plainly that help exists and that it works, because many people assume it is too late or too long ago. For yourself, talk to a therapist or your doctor if you are not sleeping, if the images will not leave you, or if it set off something from your own life. And if they ever tell you they are thinking of ending their life, stay with them and get help the same day. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Trauma', 'Boundaries']::text[], 0, '2026-10-28T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('spending-money-on-yourself', 'Spending Money on Yourself', 'You can buy for everyone else and freeze at anything for you. Where that comes from, how to tell real constraint from a rule you absorbed, and how to spend without the guilt tax.', 'You will buy your daughter the good shoes without a second thought. You will cover the meal, pay for the trip, replace the thing your partner broke. And then you stand in a shop holding a coat you need, for a price you can afford, and put it back.

Or you buy it and spend the next week feeling faintly sick about it, which is the same thing with extra steps.

Work out whether it is money or a rule

The first job is honesty about which problem this is, because they need completely different handling.

Sometimes it is genuinely money. If the account is empty, if there is debt with interest running, if the month does not reach the end, then not spending on yourself is accurate rather than a hang-up, and the real work is practical: free debt advice, benefits you may not be claiming, a conversation with the people you owe.

But if the number is affordable, the decision is boring on paper, and you still cannot do it, this is not arithmetic. It is a rule, and the rule has a history.

Where the rule usually comes from

Growing up where money was tight and anything for yourself was selfish. A parent who went without visibly and often. Being told you were spoiled. Having needed to be no trouble. A stretch of real hardship where every purchase was a risk, which left a habit that outlived the circumstances.

Or the quieter version: a belief that you have not earned it, that the money should go to people who matter more, that comfort is something other people are allowed.

That is worth naming plainly, because the rule is not about money at all. It is about who counts.

Notice the double standard

Here is the test that moves people fastest. Would you let your best friend walk around in a coat that does not keep them warm, to save ninety dollars they had?

Almost nobody says yes. Then ask why the answer is different for you, and sit with the fact that there is no good answer.

You are not being prudent when you do this. You are applying a harsher standard to yourself than you would tolerate anyone applying to someone you love.

Decide in advance rather than in the moment

Deciding at the checkout is where the guilt wins, because the shame is loudest when the money is about to leave.

So decide beforehand, calmly, when nothing is in your hand. Set a monthly amount that is for you, no justification required, and make it small enough to be plainly safe: whatever the honest version of that is for your situation.

Then move it out of the main account on payday, so it exists as a separate thing rather than as money you are taking from the household.

The amount matters less than this: the decision is already made, so the shop is not the place where it gets argued.

Spend it on the right things

People in this pattern, when they do spend, often buy a treat and then feel worse, because a treat can be denied as frivolous afterward.

Start with the things that are hard to argue with. What is broken, worn out, or hurting you: the shoes, the mattress, the dentist, the glasses, the coat. Then the ones that buy you time or relief: a cab home when you are exhausted, a cleaner once a month, the food delivery in a hard week.

Then, when those stop feeling radical, the plain pleasures. The book, the haircut, the plant, the ticket.

Watch for the two ways it comes out sideways

The guilt rarely stays put. It usually appears as one of these.

Buying the cheapest version of everything, which costs more over a few years and leaves you quietly uncomfortable the whole time.

Occasional bursts of spending that frighten you, followed by a clampdown, followed by another burst, because permanent denial is not sustainable and the pressure has to go somewhere.

A planned amount prevents both, which is most of its value.

Say the sentence out loud

I am allowed to spend money on myself. It sounds absurd written down and it is doing real work, because the belief it is answering was absorbed rather than reasoned, and reasoning alone does not shift it.

A version with specifics is better: this coat costs eighty dollars, I can afford eighty dollars, and I am going to be warm this winter.

Talk to the person you share money with

Couples often discover they have been operating on two different unspoken rules, and that one of them has been quietly going without while the other had no idea.

A plain conversation helps: we each get this much a month that the other does not get a say in. That arrangement prevents a surprising number of arguments and a great deal of resentment.

When to get help

If money is genuinely the problem, a free debt or benefits adviser is the right call and asking is not an admission of anything. If the problem is the guilt rather than the money, a therapist is worth it, especially where this goes back to how you grew up or where it sits alongside feeling you do not deserve much generally. And please talk to your doctor if money worry is keeping you awake or has pulled your mood down for weeks, because that is treatable and it is not a character failing.', array['Money', 'Self-Compassion']::text[], 0, '2026-10-28T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
